&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The WA-BC Chapter of the American Fisheries Society (WA-BC AFS) and the Washington Chapter of The Wildlife Society (WATWS) to Host a Pinniped Interactions Symposium at Joint Annual Meeting&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,sans-serif; font-size:11pt"&gt;WENATCHEE, WA, USA &ndash; As part of their Joint Annual Meeting, the WA-BC Chapter of the American Fisheries Society (WA-BC AFS) and the Washington Chapter of The Wildlife Society (WATWS) will be hosting a Pinniped Interactions Symposium on the afternoon of April 7th and morning of April 8th in Wenatchee, WA. This forum is intended to provide the most up-to-date scientific guidance related to interactions between pinnipeds and sensitive fish populations in the Pacific Northwest and beyond.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Session Abstract&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,sans-serif; font-size:11pt"&gt;Seals and sea lions (collectively known as pinnipeds) play a large role in our marine and aquatic ecosystems, acting as predator and prey for various species. Though populations were once severely depleted due to human impact, numbers seem to have generally increased in the past two decades. Given that these animals are sheltered under the Marine Mammal Protection Act (MMPA), negative interactions between humans and pinnipeds are avoided whenever possible. However, this becomes challenging in instances such as: where pinnipeds predate on threatened/endangered fish populations particularly at passage &ldquo;bottlenecks&rdquo; in riverine environments, when they are caught as bycatch during fisheries operations, or when they interact with aquaculture facilities leading to escapements and loss of profit. The purpose of this symposium* is to identify the best available research quantifying such interactions, connect those interested in the topic, and gather solutions that prevent conflict.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,sans-serif; font-size:11pt"&gt;*Note: Though pinnipeds are the main focus of this symposium, relevant presentations referencing other marine mammal species interactions can also be included in this session.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Information for Attendees&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

